    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Los Ojos was founded in 1860, making it one of the older settlements in this part of the territory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.yourlocalsquare.com/blog/los-ojos-nm-87551"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Los Ojos means \"the springs or the eyes\" in Spanish, a reference to the natural springs that once drew settlers to this valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.yourlocalsquare.com/blog/los-ojos-nm-87551"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Cumbres & Toltec Scenic Railroad, accessible from nearby Chama, is a National Historic Landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.yourlocalsquare.com/blog/los-ojos-nm-87551"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Tierra Wools, founded in 1983 by Ganados del Valle, preserves Rio Grande weaving traditions that span more than 200 years of Spanish heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.yourlocalsquare.com/blog/los-ojos-nm-87551"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Historically, the area functioned as a stagecoach stop along the route between Santa Fé and Durango, Colorado, supporting early overland travel in the late 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/los_ojos_new_mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Los Ojos sits at 7,372 feet, a high point in Rio Arriba County that echoes its name meaning \"Upper River\".",
